 		<English>[H1]AI Personality Aggregates (AdvCiv-SAS)[\H1]Note: Doc written by ChatGPT (and full AI Aggregates code also comes from ChatGPT, i only implemented it (copy paste) after prompting it (and did the table and such in python with ChatGPT's help but anyways)). As for an example to see it, see [LINK=LEADER_GANDHI]Gandhi's leader page for example[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2]AI Personality Aggregates are a unique feature of AdvCiv-SAS that provide insight into how each leader behaves, beyond visible traits like Aggressive or Creative. They are based on hidden AI values found in CIV4LeaderHeadInfos.xml, such as MaxWarRand, BuildUnitProb, PeaceWeight, and many others. These values are normally invisible during gameplay but strongly influence how each AI leader acts in diplomacy, war, trade, and empire management.[PARAGRAPH:1]Aggregates are computed by combining multiple of these hidden values into a single number between 0-100. This number is normalized relative to all other leaders using a min-max formula. For example, if the lowest value among all leaders is 10 and the highest is 50, then a leader with 30 would score 50 on the normalized scale. The point is to allow you to compare leaders easily on a single axis like "Aggressiveness" or "Builder Tendency".[PARAGRAPH:1]Each behavior group - called an aggregate - consists of a name, a score (0-100), and a visual scale made of symbols such as ### or #####. All aggregates use the same symbol format (#) for readability. The score reflects how strongly a leader expresses the associated behavior, after normalizing relevant AI attributes. In most cases, a higher score means the leader behaves more intensely in that area. However, some attributes are inversed before aggregation, such as when a lower value leads to stronger behavior (e.g. lower WonderConstructRand means more likely to build wonders). These inversions are handled automatically in the calculation. The result is a clean, intuitive display where higher scores always mean more of the defined behavior.[PARAGRAPH:1]Let us walk through one example. The "Sneaky" trait is calculated using three XML values: getDemandRebukedSneakProb, getEspionageWeight, and getDogpileWarRand. For each of these, the AI has a number in the XML - for instance, getEspionageWeight might be 40. We collect the values for all leaders, then normalize the selected leader's value to a number between 0-100. If one of the XML values is inverted (meaning a lower number makes the AI more inclined to do something, like lower WonderConstructRand means more wonder-building), then the normalization is reversed for that value before averaging.[PARAGRAPH:1]Then we average the three normalized values, and the result is the final aggregate score. So if a leader has a sneak prob of 80/100, espionage of 60/100, and dogpile tendency of 40/100, the result would be 60/100 on "Sneaky". That is what gets displayed in the AI Personality panel.[PARAGRAPH:1]These aggregates are shown on the right side of the Sevopedia Leader page. Each entry shows the name of the trait, the computed score, and the visual scale. The traits are grouped into categories like War Strategy, Diplomacy, Economic Preferences, Victory Strategy, Attitude Modifiers, Trade Thresholds, and Aggregates.[PARAGRAPH:1]Aggregates are new, and they are not part of standard Civilization IV or AdvCiv. They were designed fully by ChatGPT, including the Python logic and the display strategy. All the math is done using Python inside SevoPediaLeader.py, and the values are pulled live from the XML data already defined by the game. That means they work for all existing leaders and any custom leaders you add - as long as those leaders have AI values, the system will work for them too.[PARAGRAPH:1]As a modder or player, you can use this information to understand the AI better. If an AI has high "Stubborn", you know that even at Friendly they may refuse your deals. If another has high "Dogpiler", be careful when you are weak in a war - they might declare on you opportunistically. Traits like "Builder", "Flexible", or "Tech Hoarder" give you clues about their priorities.[PARAGRAPH:1]This system does not override anything in the game - it simply makes visible what was already there but hidden. It helps with both immersion and gameplay understanding. The traits are not made up: they are just summaries of existing AI behavior, now available in a readable form.[PARAGRAPH:1]You can edit the behavior formulas directly in Python. Every trait is defined as a list of XML getters, each with an optional inverse flag. Modders can create new traits or adjust the weighting as they see fit. The layout is customizable too, but even without edits, the system is plug-and-play.[PARAGRAPH:1]In conclusion, AI Personality Aggregates are a powerful addition for any player or modder who wants to understand the AI beyond simple trait labels. They show you how an AI is wired, what it values, and how it is likely to behave in a variety of situations. For learning, planning, diplomacy, or modding - they are an invaluable tool.[PARAGRAPH:2]The aggregates listed below are organized by their underlying behavior themes. While the in-game display does not explicitly group them into categories (to conserve space), they are logically grouped in the code and in this documentation for clarity. Each entry includes a definition, the XML values used to compute it, an explanation of how the score is derived, and what it means for gameplay and strategy. This information can help players understand not only how an AI might behave in isolation, but also how its behaviors interact with others on the map.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][H1]Combat and Military Behavior[\H1]- Aggressive: Reflects a leader's general willingness to wage war. High values mean frequent war declarations, stronger offensive priorities, and a tendency to build more military units. Computed using getMaxWarRand, getBuildUnitProb, and getBaseAttackOddsChange. Higher scores indicate a more aggressive leader that poses military threats throughout the game.[NEWLINE]- Dogpiler: Measures how likely a leader is to join existing wars against weakened players. Includes getDogpileWarRand, getDeclareWarTradeRand, and getWarmongerRespect. High values signal a tendency to opportunistically jump into wars, which means players weakened by ongoing wars should be wary of this AI.[NEWLINE]- Reckless: Captures how risky and unpredictable a leader is in war-related decisions. Based on getBaseAttackOddsChange, getLimitedWarRand, and getDeclareWarRefuseAttitudeThreshold (inverted). High values mean the AI may take risky battles, start wars with low justification, and disregard diplomatic warnings.[NEWLINE]- Allied Warmonger: Represents AIs who combine strong military behavior with collaborative war efforts. Uses getWarmongerRespect, getShareWarAttitudeChangeLimit, and getDogpileWarRand. These AIs will team up in wars and are more dangerous in multiplayer AI team-ups.[NEWLINE]- Border Watcher: Focuses on leaders that are sensitive to nearby borders and land ownership. Uses getMaxWarMinAdjacentLandPercent, getCloseBordersAttitudeChange, and getMaxWarNearbyPowerRatio. High scores mean the AI dislikes neighbors and may act to protect or expand its borders aggressively.[NEWLINE]- Power Tracker: Tracks how much the AI pays attention to power ratios before engaging in war. Computed with getMaxWarNearbyPowerRatio, getMaxWarDistantPowerRatio, and getLimitedWarPowerRatio. A high score indicates a calculated war planner that only strikes when strong.[NEWLINE][H1]Diplomatic Behavior[\H1]- Diplomatic: A measure of peaceful, cooperation-prone behavior. Built from getBasePeaceWeight, getNoTechTradeThreshold (inverted), and getOpenBordersRefuseAttitudeThreshold (inverted). High scores mean more open borders, tech trades, and diplomacy.[NEWLINE]- Isolationist: Opposite of Diplomatic. Uses getOpenBordersRefuseAttitudeThreshold, getDeclareWarRefuseAttitudeThreshold, and getStopTradingRefuseAttitudeThreshold. High values mean this leader avoids interaction, is hard to befriend, and rarely opens up diplomatically.[NEWLINE]- Stubborn: Reflects general refusal behavior, especially in diplomacy and trade. Includes getTechRefuseAttitudeThreshold, getCityRefuseAttitudeThreshold, getStopTradingRefuseAttitudeThreshold, getAdoptCivicRefuseAttitudeThreshold, and getConvertReligionRefuseAttitudeThreshold. These AIs are hard to deal with even at Friendly attitudes.[NEWLINE]- Dealbreaker: A more focused variant of "Stubborn,"" covering deal-breaking behaviors. Uses getRefuseToTalkWarThreshold, getTechRefuseAttitudeThreshold, and getStopTradingRefuseAttitudeThreshold. High values mean the AI often refuses negotiations, even when a player expects cooperation.[NEWLINE]- Grudgy: Captures long memory for slights and border tension. Based on getWorseRankDifferenceAttitudeChange, getCloseBordersAttitudeChange, and getDifferentReligionAttitudeChangeLimit. High scores indicate that once annoyed, the leader will stay that way and hold grudges.[NEWLINE]- Collaborative: Measures openness to cooperative diplomatic modifiers. Computed from getShareWarAttitudeChangeLimit, getSameReligionAttitudeChangeLimit, and getFavoriteCivicAttitudeChangeLimit. High scores mean the leader rewards alignment in civics, religions, and shared enemies.[NEWLINE][H1]Economic and Strategic Planning[\H1]- Builder: Measures inclination toward peaceful infrastructure. Based on getWonderConstructRand (inverted), getBuildUnitProb (inverted), and getMaxGoldTradePercent. High values indicate leaders that focus on wonders, economy, and long-term growth.[NEWLINE]- Trader: Focuses on openness to trade and mutual benefit. Uses getTechTradeKnownPercent, getMaxGoldTradePercent, and getMapRefuseAttitudeThreshold (inverted). These leaders frequently trade maps, techs, and gold with others.[NEWLINE]- Gold Hoarder: Tracks leaders who collect and guard gold. Includes getMaxGoldTradePercent, getMaxGoldPerTurnTradePercent, and getCityRefuseAttitudeThreshold. High values mean they build a strong treasury but don't like to share it.[NEWLINE]- Tech Hoarder: Measures reluctance to share technology. Based on getNoTechTradeThreshold, getTechRefuseAttitudeThreshold, and getTechTradeKnownPercent (inverted). High scores mean tech trades are rare and expensive.[NEWLINE]- Micro-manager: Captures small-scale detail preferences. Uses getAttackOddsChangeRand, getMakePeaceRand, and getRefuseToTalkWarThreshold. These AIs make cautious but intricate decisions.[NEWLINE][H1]Behavioral Nuance and Opportunism[\H1]- Sneaky: Represents trickiness and opportunism. Built from getDemandRebukedSneakProb, getDogpileWarRand, and getEspionageWeight. Leaders with high scores are more likely to backstab or conduct covert ops.[NEWLINE]- Opportunistic: Tracks general opportunism in trade and war. Computed from getDeclareWarTradeRand, getTechTradeKnownPercent, and getWarmongerRespect. Leaders with high scores adjust rapidly to power shifts and tech advantages.[NEWLINE]- Flexible: Indicates diplomatic and civic adaptability. Uses getAdoptCivicRefuseAttitudeThreshold (inverted), getConvertReligionRefuseAttitudeThreshold (inverted), and getFavoriteCivicAttitudeChangeLimit (inverted). High scores mean they are more likely to accommodate player demands.[NEWLINE]- Vassalizer: Tracks use of vassal mechanics. Based on getVassalRefuseAttitudeThreshold, getPowerWeightModifier, and getRefuseToTalkWarThreshold (inverted). These AIs seek to dominate and subdue rather than destroy.[NEWLINE]- Culture Pusher: Leaders focused on cultural victories and ideological influence. Computed with getCultureVictoryWeight, getMaxWarMinAdjacentLandPercent (inverted), and getSameReligionAttitudeChangeLimit. High values indicate passive expansion and border pressure.[NEWLINE]- Wonder Chaser: Combines builder logic with prestige focus. Uses getWonderConstructRand (inverted), getBuildUnitProb (inverted), and getBasePeaceWeight. These AIs will prioritize wonders and prestige structures, but may sacrifice military for it.[NEWLINE]- Unpredictable: Captures erratic or random tendencies. Computed with getAttackOddsChangeRand, getLimitedWarRand, and getDeclareWarTradeRand. High scores mean they can surprise players � for better or worse.[PARAGRAPH:2]In addition to aggregated traits, each leader also has individual AI personality attributes. These are hidden XML values that directly control how an AI behaves in war, diplomacy, trade, development, and overall strategy. Each attribute influences one specific behavior. Most values are located in CIV4LeaderHeadInfos.xml. The following list explains each known attribute, how it works, and what it means in terms of gameplay. These values are visible in the AI Personality panel for each leader in Sevopedia.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]- Max War Rand: This value determines how often the AI will consider declaring a full war. A higher value makes the AI less likely to start wars. Lower values indicate a war-prone leader. This is one of the strongest indicators of military aggressiveness.[NEWLINE]- Max War Nearby Power Ratio: This is the power threshold the AI checks before declaring war on a nearby opponent. A lower ratio means the AI is more willing to attack even when the target is strong. A higher ratio means the AI prefers to attack only when it has superior strength.[NEWLINE]- Max War Distant Power Ratio: Similar to the previous attribute, but for distant targets. A high value makes the AI less likely to attack civilizations far away. This controls overseas invasions and distant wars.[NEWLINE]- Max War Min Adjacent Land Percent: This controls whether the AI considers launching a full war based on how much border it shares with a rival. A higher value means the AI needs a large border to consider attacking. A lower value means it is willing to attack with only limited land access.[NEWLINE]- Limited War Rand: Determines how often the AI will consider limited wars (small-scale or opportunistic attacks). Lower values mean the AI will try this more often.[NEWLINE]- Limited War Power Ratio: Similar to the Max War ratio, but for limited wars. A low value makes the AI more likely to attack even if it is weaker.[NEWLINE]- Dogpile War Rand: This determines how likely an AI is to join an existing war against a player or civ who is already under attack. Low values make it more likely to dogpile. AIs with low values here are dangerous during wars where you're already losing.[NEWLINE]- Make Peace Rand: Controls how often the AI will consider making peace. A higher number means peace is less likely. AIs with low numbers here may be more open to ending wars early.[NEWLINE]- Base Attitude: A baseline modifier to the AI's attitude toward other players. This is usually 0, but can be positive or negative. It affects diplomatic relations passively.[NEWLINE]- Peace Weight: Indicates how peaceful the AI is overall. High values mean the AI prefers peace and dislikes warmongers. Low values mean the AI is indifferent to war or may admire aggression. This also influences how it votes in the UN and AP.[NEWLINE]- Warmonger Respect: How much the AI respects or fears warmongers. Higher values mean it will like aggressive leaders more. Low values mean it will dislike or avoid them.[NEWLINE]- Demand Rebuked Sneak Prob: The chance the AI will launch a sneak attack after you refuse a demand. High values mean they may surprise-attack you if you deny them.[NEWLINE]- Demand Rebuked War Prob: Similar to the previous value, but for open war declaration (not sneak attack). Low values mean fewer consequences when denying demands.[NEWLINE]- Refuse to Talk War Threshold: This value controls how long an AI refuses to talk during war. Higher values mean longer periods of isolation. Affects your ability to negotiate peace.[NEWLINE]- No Tech Trade Threshold: Controls how willing the AI is to trade technology. Higher values mean tech hoarding. Lower values mean openness to tech exchange.[NEWLINE]- Tech Trade Known Percent: The minimum percent of civilizations that must know a tech before the AI is willing to trade it. High values mean the AI waits until everyone knows it.[NEWLINE]- Declare War Trade Rand: Determines the AI's willingness to declare war on demand through diplomacy. Lower values mean it's easier to bribe them into attacking others.[NEWLINE]- Espionage Weight: How much focus the AI puts on espionage missions. Higher values mean more spies, sabotage, and counter-intelligence. Affects gameplay especially with espionage-enabled victory conditions or sabotage threats.[NEWLINE]- Build Unit Prob: Probability that the AI will choose to build military units over civilian ones. Higher values mean a stronger military buildup. Affects unit spam and wartime resilience.[NEWLINE]- Base Attack Odds Change: Increases or decreases the AI's estimated odds for attacking. Higher values make them more aggressive. Can lead to reckless combat. Lower values make them more cautious.[NEWLINE]- Attack Odds Change Rand: Adds randomness to the AI's decision to attack. Higher values introduce more variability in combat choices. Can make the AI seem less predictable in battle.[NEWLINE]- Wonder Construct Rand: Affects how likely the AI is to build wonders. Lower values mean more focus on wonder construction. This also affects how competitive the AI is for key buildings like the Great Library or Pyramids.[NEWLINE]- Max Gold Trade Percent: Controls how much gold the AI is willing to trade in a single deal. Higher values mean more generous offers. Affects diplomacy and AI flexibility in trades.[NEWLINE]- Max Gold Per Turn Trade Percent: Same as above, but for GPT (gold per turn) deals. Higher values mean the AI is more willing to commit to long-term trades.[NEWLINE]- Worse Rank Difference Attitude Change: How much the AI's attitude worsens toward civs ranked lower than them. High values indicate arrogance. Leaders with high values may look down on weaker civs.[NEWLINE]- Better Rank Difference Attitude Change: How much the AI dislikes stronger civs. High values indicate envy or fear. These leaders are more suspicious of top players.[NEWLINE]- Close Borders Attitude Change: Penalty applied when borders are too close. Affects peaceful coexistence. Higher values mean the AI dislikes you more for crowding them.[NEWLINE]- Same Religion Attitude Change Limit: Bonus to attitude for sharing religion. Higher values make the AI favor you more if you have the same faith. Useful for diplomacy and alliances.[NEWLINE]- Different Religion Attitude Change Limit: Penalty to attitude when religions differ. Higher values make the AI more intolerant. Can sabotage relations even with friendly leaders.[NEWLINE]- Share War Attitude Change Limit: Bonus to attitude for fighting the same enemy. High values make the AI more appreciative of joint wars. Can lead to long-term allies.[NEWLINE]- Favorite Civic Attitude Change Limit: Bonus to attitude for using their favorite civic. Helps identify what civics to adopt to get in their good graces.[NEWLINE]- Tech Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude required for the AI to trade tech. Lower values mean they are more willing to trade even if they dislike you. High values mean they require Friendly or better.[NEWLINE]- City Refuse Attitude Threshold: Same as above, but for city trades. AIs rarely trade cities, but this makes it even harder or easier depending on the number.[NEWLINE]- Strategic Bonus Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude required to trade strategic resources (copper, iron, horses). High values mean they are stingy.[NEWLINE]- Health Bonus Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude to trade health resources (corn, pigs, etc.).[NEWLINE]- Happiness Bonus Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude to trade happiness resources (gems, incense, etc.).[NEWLINE]- Map Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude to share maps. Low values mean they are more willing to cooperate with cartography and reveal information.[NEWLINE]- Declare War Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude required to agree to declare war on someone. Lower is better if you want to bribe them into war.[NEWLINE]- Declare War Them Refuse Attitude Threshold: Same as above, but for declaring war on someone the AI is currently trading with or has special relations with.[NEWLINE]- Stop Trading Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude required to ask the AI to stop trading with a third party. Higher values mean more loyalty to existing partners.[NEWLINE]- Stop Trading Them Refuse Attitude Threshold: Variant of the previous value. Affects refusal when the AI likes the third party.[NEWLINE]- Adopt Civic Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude required to ask the AI to switch to your preferred civic. High values mean they rarely switch. Affects diplomatic civics requests.[NEWLINE]- Convert Religion Refuse Attitude Threshold: Same as above but for religion. AIs with high values here are religiously stubborn.[NEWLINE]- Open Borders Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude to accept an open borders agreement. High values mean isolationist leaders who refuse trade routes or passage.[NEWLINE]- Vassal Refuse Attitude Threshold: Minimum attitude needed to accept vassalage. Affects capitulation and voluntary vassal offers. High values make diplomacy harder.[NEWLINE]- Power Weight Modifier: A rarely used but powerful modifier that increases or decreases how much military power affects AI evaluations. Higher values make the AI more responsive to power ratios (yours or theirs).[PARAGRAPH:2]In addition to the core AI personality attributes listed above, leaders in Civilization IV also possess a wide array of secondary behavioral modifiers defined in CIV4LeaderHeadInfos.xml. These values do not appear in the AI Personality panel but still influence AI behavior behind the scenes. They include diplomatic contact probabilities, memory-based attitude changes, war aversion levels, unit and improvement preferences, and flavor weights for strategic tendencies. For example, ContactRands like CONTACT_TRADE_TECH or CONTACT_DEMAND_TRIBUTE determine how frequently an AI initiates certain diplomatic actions. MemoryAttitudePercents control how long an AI remembers events like tech trades or betrayals. NoWarAttitudeProbs adjust the AI's likelihood of refusing to declare war based on its attitude (e.g. Cautious or Pleased). UnitAIWeightModifiers and ImprovementWeightModifiers affect the AI's preference for assigning units to roles like UNITAI_RESERVE or building specific improvements like farms. Finally, Flavor values influence strategic priorities such as military, culture, or religion, and shape the AI's behavior in broader gameplay contexts. While these attributes are not visualized in-game, they play a vital role in shaping AI depth and realism. Modders may wish to inspect and adjust these fields to fine-tune personality profiles beyond the visible attributes. A future section may document these values in more detail, including practical examples and modding guidance.[NEWLINE]</English>
